11
00:36:32,142 --> 00:36:38,142
The Wicker Man is a 1973 British folk horror film directed by Robin Hardy and starring
Edward Woodward, Britt Ekland, Diane Cilento, Ingrid Pitt and Christopher Lee.

12
00:39:51,428 --> 00:39:57,428
The screenplay is by Anthony Shaffer, inspired by David Pinner's 1967
novel Ritual, and Paul Giovanni composed the film score.

13
00:43:10,714 --> 00:43:16,714
The plot centres on the visit of a police officer, Sergeant Neil Howie, to the
fictional, isolated Scottish island of Summerisle in search of a missing girl.

14
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:36,000
Howie, a devout Christian, is appalled to find that the inhabitants of the island
have abandoned Christianity and now practise a form of Celtic paganism.

15
00:49:49,285 --> 00:49:55,285
The Wicker Man is well regarded by critics.

16
00:53:08,571 --> 00:53:14,571
Film magazine Cinefantastique described it as "The Citizen Kane of horror movies", and in 2004,
Total Film magazine named The Wicker Man the sixth-greatest British film of all time.

17
00:56:27,857 --> 00:56:33,857
It also won the 1978 Saturn Award for Best Horror Film.

18
00:59:47,142 --> 00:59:53,142
The final scene was number 45 on Bravo's 100 Scariest Movie Moments, and during the 2012 Summer Olympics
opening ceremony, it was included as part of a sequence that celebrated British cinema.

19
01:03:06,428 --> 01:03:12,428
The film brought the wicker man into modern popular culture.

20
01:06:25,714 --> 01:06:31,714
In 1989, Shaffer wrote a script treatment for The Loathsome
Lambton Worm, a direct sequel with fantasy elements.

21
01:09:45,000 --> 01:09:51,000
Hardy had no interest in the project, and it went unproduced.

22
01:13:04,285 --> 01:13:10,285
In 2006, a poorly received American remake starring Nicolas Cage was released, from which
Hardy and others involved with the original have dissociated themselves.

23
01:16:23,571 --> 01:16:29,571
In 2011, a spiritual sequel written and directed by Hardy, The Wicker
Tree, was released; it featured Lee in a cameo appearance.

24
01:19:42,857 --> 01:19:48,857
In 2013, the original U.S.

25
01:23:02,142 --> 01:23:08,142
theatrical version of The Wicker Man was digitally restored and released.

26
01:26:21,428 --> 01:26:27,428
The film explores themes of virgin, island, based on
novel or book, sacrifice, scotland.

27
01:29:40,714 --> 01:29:46,714
The tone is dark.
